<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mpande</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1798–<a href="/years/1872">1872</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mpande kaSenzangakhona</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mpande kaSenzangakhona was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> from 1840 to 1872, a half-brother of Shaka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a>. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> support he overthrew Dingane in 1840 and went on to reign longer than any other Zulu king, keeping peace with the British in Natal at the cost of the land south of the Thukela. Late in his reign his sons fought for the succession, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbuyazi">Mbuyazi</a> in 1856.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mpande.jpg" alt="Mpande" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King-Mpande-1.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Relations with the Zulu were the main threat: Zulu armies attacked the Swazi in the 1830s and again under <b>Mpande</b> in the 1840s, and Mswati sought support from the British in Natal and from Boer settlers at Lydenburg, to whom he ceded land in 1846 and 1855.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swazi-kingdom">Swazi Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His half-brother <b>Mpande</b>, with Boer support, overthrew him in 1840 and ruled for more than three decades, a period of relative stability in which the kingdom accepted the loss of land south of the Thukela to the British Colony of Natal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The throne passed by force more often than by rule: Dingane and Mhlangana murdered Shaka in 1828, <b>Mpande</b> overthrew Dingane with Voortrekker support, and Cetshwayo defeated his brother Mbuyazi at Ndondakusuka in 1856 and took over his father’s authority long before Mpande died in 1872.</blockquote><a href="/societies/zulu-kingdom">Social Hierarchy of the Zulu Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1840 the Boers helped <b>Mpande</b> overthrow Dingane and seized thousands of cattle from the Zulu Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/natalia-republic">Natalia Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1854 Boer farmers obtained a strip of land along the Buffalo River on the Zulu border from King <b>Mpande</b> and founded the village of Utrecht, organising themselves as a small independent republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-utrecht">Republic of Utrecht</a></li></ul></section>
